    source: 'Public Trade Show Demo',
    era: '2000s',
    description: 'Shown at Nintendo Space World 2000, Super Mario 128 put 128 Marios on screen at once and was never released as a game. Its ideas ended up scattered across Pikmin, Metroid Prime, Twilight Princess and Super Mario Galaxy.',
    longDescription: 'At Nintendo Space World in August 2000, Nintendo demonstrated the raw capability of the forthcoming GameCube with a technical showcase called Super Mario 128. It did what its name promised: 128 individually simulated Marios stood on a circular platform, marched about, assembled themselves into a giant Mario mosaic, and deformed the terrain beneath them — which could be raised, lowered, and at one point folded into a pizza. It was a physics and AI stress test wearing a Mario costume, and Shigeru Miyamoto said as much at the time. The gaming press did not listen. For six years "Super Mario 128" was reported as an imminent Mario sequel, and its non-appearance became one of the era\'s great vapourware sagas. The name had form: Nintendo had already used it internally in 1997 for a cancelled 64DD follow-up to Super Mario 64. Miyamoto finally confirmed the project\'s cancellation in 2006, and at GDC 2007 explained where it had gone — not into a game, but into several. The crowd-AI work resurfaced as Pikmin; the walk-on-a-sphere geometry became the defining mechanic of Super Mario Galaxy. Nothing playable has ever leaked.',
