    longDescription: 'The intrusion was not sophisticated, which is part of what made it so damaging. Axel Gembe, a young German programmer whose favourite game was Half-Life, found that Valve\'s name server permitted anonymous zone transfers — meaning it would simply hand over a list of every subdomain the company operated to anyone who asked. From there he found a domain controller carrying an account named "build" with no password on it. On 19 September 2003 he downloaded the unfinished game. The Source engine source code was posted publicly on 2 October and an incomplete build of the game itself on 7 October, thirteen months before Half-Life 2 shipped. The community name for what circulated — the "Half-Life 2 beta" — is a convenient misnomer. What leaked was not a single coherent build but files spanning years of development, which is why the leak is so revealing and so unplayable at the same time: whole chapters exist in fragments, maps reference assets that were removed at different points, and a great deal of it cannot be assembled into anything that runs end to end. What it does contain is a record of the game Valve was making before it made the one that shipped: a darker, more industrial City 17, and a body of cut material including the Air Exchange and Kraken Base chapters and enemies such as the Hydra and the Combine Guard. Valve\'s response was to ask for help — Gabe Newell posted publicly to the Half-Life community appealing for information about the intruder, an unusual move that worked. Gembe was identified, and the pursuit that followed took an unusual turn: he was reportedly offered a job interview in the United States, an arrangement that would have delivered him to the FBI, before German authorities acted first. He admitted infiltrating Valve — it was never established that he was the one who published the files — and received a two-year probationary sentence.',
